[Unit]
Description=Weekly (Monday) timer for the egress two-arm canary window (#2555; bounded by #2770)
# WHEN IT FIRES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT AVOID.
#
# Weekly, Mondays at 06:23 local (13:23 UTC). Two numbers, both read off the other units in
# this directory —
# which is the only time table this box has, because the autonomy jobs declare symbolic
# frequencies (`frequency: daily` x28, `weekly` x8, `every-15min`, `6x-daily`, and 4 files
# with none, across the 42 `~/obsidian/autonomy/*.md` measured 2026-10-11) and carry no cron
# minute field at all. `tests/test_egress_window_unit.py::
# test_the_timer_hour_is_after_every_named_fire_and_clear_of_the_sibling_ceiling` re-reads
# every `.timer` beside this one and re-derives the sibling's ceiling, so this paragraph
# cannot rot into a lie the way `agent-services/systemd/lloyd-persistence-window.service`'s
# bare line-number citation for the queue's pool-status query did: it pointed far above the
# def it meant, shipped unnoticed, and #2772 replaced it with the symbol pointer that
# tests/test_egress_window_unit.py now resolves for every unit in this directory.
#
# THE HOUR — after every scheduled thing this directory names, and clear of the OTHER
# measurement window even at its own ceiling. The two calendars here that still fire every
# day do so at 04:17 (cert-renew) and 04:45 (qmd-cleanup), and SETUP.md puts the named
# autonomy jobs at 04:00 health report, 05:00 qmd index maintenance, 05:30 graph backup and
# 06:00 retrieval eval; 06:23 sits behind all of them. `lloyd-persistence-window.timer`
# fires at 02:10 on Mondays (#2754) and its service bounds the run with `TimeoutStartSec=4h`,
# so 02:10 + 4 h puts the latest that window can still be holding the engine at 06:10:
# starting 13 minutes past that ceiling is what keeps the two windows' engine work from
# overlapping, and that is arithmetic rather than a guess. The remaining timers are hourly
# at :00 (data-snapshot), every 15 minutes (vault-backup, and `lloyd-guardian-nag.timer`
# from boot), so none lands on :23 either.
#
# THE MINUTES — :23 is a minute no repo timer fires on, and `RandomizedDelaySec` below can
# add at most 300 s to it, so the real start stays inside :23–:28, the same quarter-hour as
# the calendar. The sibling sits at :10 precisely so the 15-minute vault backup at :00/:15/:30
# cannot overlap it, and the same logic says a window must not be pushed onto a :00 or a :30.
#
# WHAT THIS HOUR DOES NOT DO — it does not dodge automod rounds, and the unit must not be
# believed if it reads that way. Measured 2026-10-11 from workers.db over 2026-10-04 →
# 10-11: 420 `autocode` rounds, resident at 67.2% of 5-minute samples, with starts in every
# one of the 24 UTC hours (29 to 51 per hour) and duration p95 54.5 min against the driver's
# 900 s default drain. Rounds are a near-constant presence, not a scheduled one, so no fire
# time avoids them and the calendar is not the safety mechanism. The lever that works is the
# explicit `--drain-wait 3600` in `lloyd-egress-window.service`, which exceeds the p95
# measured here and lets the window wait a round out instead of refusing (which is how
# `lloyd-persistence-window.service`'s first timer run died at 02:26:39-07:00 with
# `{'in_flight': 1, 'autocode:running': 1}` — the queue's pool-status query is
# `depth_by_source()` at workers/queue.py:1253, which counts the window's own row because
# there is nothing excluding it).

[Timer]
# Weekly Mondays — the cadence #2754 gave `lloyd-persistence-window.timer`
# (`Mon *-*-* 02:10:00`) and #2770 extends here: precedent, not a new measurement, and
# cadence is all that moves. Both windows now fire on the same weekday, so the
# 02:10 + 4 h = 06:10 latest-hold arithmetic above is what makes Monday's two windows
# consecutive rather than concurrent: this one starts 13 minutes past the sibling's
# ceiling, and the 300 s jitter below keeps the real start inside :23–:28. The other
# six days carry neither window.
# The `Mon` is the cadence and the date field stays deliberately wild: `*-*-1 06:23:00`
# reads like a weekly spec but puts the `1` in the day-of-MONTH field, and systemd
# normalizes it to `*-*-01` — firing on the 1st of each month, twelve times a year of a
# window that means fifty-two. Asserted against systemd's own parser by
# tests/test_egress_window_unit.py::
# test_systemd_itself_reads_the_cadence_as_weekly_not_monthly.
OnCalendar=Mon *-*-* 06:23:00
# One rep x the 18 shipped scenarios x both arms is the sample the per-arm pair is published
# from; more is hours of the primary held by a canary.
Persistent=true
# Bounded: 300 s keeps the real start inside :23–:28. A 600 s jitter is the ceiling the
# paragraph above reasons against — it can push the start to :23+:10 = :33, onto the vault
# backup's :30 — so the fire time the prose defends would not be the fire time.
RandomizedDelaySec=300

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
